# Post-analysis workflow

The analysis is "done" when:

- All planned outputs are in outputs/figures/ and outputs/processed/
- The validation gates in 40-validate.mdc pass
- The user has reviewed the figures and is happy

After that, when the user types one of:

- "wrap up" → email draft bundle only (draft + subject + attachments list)
- "wrap up + newsletter" → email bundle + newsletter commentary
  (commentary auto-attaches to the email)
- "wrap up + send" → email bundle + commentary + auto-send
- "send drafted" → SEND ONLY the files already on disk; regenerate nothing

…run the post-analysis protocol below.

## Audience register (applies to email body + newsletter)

Both the email body and the newsletter are written for a **non-technical
reader** — someone who may have little quantitative background and won't
follow heavy jargon. Simplify the language: explain plainly, gloss or avoid
technical terms.

**Simplify the language, not the facts.** Even in the accessible prose,
always preserve:

- the **workhorse model / technique by name** (with a one-clause plain gloss),
- the **sample range**, and
- the **major assumption(s)** the result hinges on.

These three are mandatory in both deliverables.

The split that lets the prose stay light:

- The **EMAIL** carries a full-rigor **Model details** section near the end
  (after caveats, before "What's attached") — equations, restrictions,
  priors, inputs/outputs, estimation + testing ranges, validation. That
  section is where jargon is welcome.
- The **NEWSLETTER** does NOT get a Model details section — it stays terse
  (<250 words), so the model/sample/assumption facts are folded into a
  sentence or two of plain prose.

Cited literature **actually used** in the project is **hyperlinked and kept
very short** (author + year) in both the email and the newsletter. Link only
to a verifiable canonical URL/DOI; never fabricate one (see "Verify any
source-paper citation").

## Required pre-step: READ THE OUTPUT FILES

Before drafting anything, actually read the output files. Use:

- pd.read_parquet / pd.read_csv on every file in outputs/processed/
- PIL.Image or just file existence checks for outputs/figures/

Extract the actual numbers. NEVER use placeholders like "[XX%]" or
"approximately X.X". The whole point of writing after the analysis is
to use the real numbers.

If outputs/ is empty or the analysis hasn't run, STOP and tell the user.

## Verify any source-paper citation

If the commentary or email cites a source paper (authors, title, year),
verify it against the local source materials in notes/ — never generate a
citation from memory. Read notes/<source>.pdf (or the relevant slice) and
match the author list, title, and year EXACTLY. Cross-check any chart
titles/footers the commentary ships, not just the prose. A hallucinated
author list is invisible to anyone who hasn't read the paper and immediately
obvious to anyone who has — the kind of error that quietly erodes trust with
the most informed readers.

**Hyperlinks get the same discipline.** The link target must be the paper's
real canonical URL/DOI — take it from the source PDF in notes/ or a
verifiable official source (DOI, NBER, SSRN, a Fed/BIS/IMF page). NEVER
fabricate or guess a URL. If you can't verify the link, ship the short cite
WITHOUT a hyperlink rather than risk a dead or wrong link.

## Email draft

This step produces the COMPLETE send-ready bundle — all three files —
so that any "wrap up" variant leaves outputs/email/ ready for "send
drafted" with no missing-file gaps. Do NOT defer subject/attachments
creation to the send step.

Use the template at:
@C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/prompts/post_analysis_email.md

Write all three of these to outputs/email/:

1. email_draft.md ← the email body, written for a non-technical reader per
   "Audience register". Body order: headline → study design (accessible,
   with the named model + sample + key assumption + short hyperlinked cite)
   → key insights → caveats (optional) → **Model details (full rigor)** →
   "What's attached". Wrap key numbers and the Model-details labels in
   <b>...</b> (HTML bold) so they survive the markdown → HTML conversion in
   the send step. Use simple HTML; no fancy CSS.

2. email_subject.txt ← the subject line, one line. If a newsletter is
   generated this run, you may reuse its suggested title; otherwise
   write a subject from the headline finding.

3. email_attachments.txt ← one file path per line (relative to project
   root or absolute), listing every file to attach. Populate from the
   analysis outputs per "Attachments" / "Charts in email vs backup"
   below. If a newsletter is generated this run, commentary.md is
   appended here by the Newsletter-commentary step.

All three must exist and be non-empty at the end of every wrap-up
variant. The "What's attached" section in email_draft.md must match
the actual contents of email_attachments.txt — keep them in sync.

### Model details section (inside email_draft.md)

After the caveats and BEFORE "What's attached", add a **Model details**
section in FULL technical rigor — the body deliberately stayed accessible;
this section is where the depth goes. Use a compact labeled block (labels in
<b>...</b>): model/technique + estimator + priors, source(s) (hyperlinked),
inputs, outputs, estimation/sample range, testing/validation range, key
assumptions/identifying restrictions, validation. Pull every line from
plan.md (§1, §5, §7) and the output files — never invent a spec detail. The
prompt template spells out the exact labels.

### "What's attached" section (inside email_draft.md)

After Model details, add a short "What's attached" section that tells the
reader how to READ each file — not a second findings section. You must
actually open the files first:
  - Figures: read the image; describe axes/units, what each line is, and
    how to read the legend. Call out multi-line or multi-panel plots
    explicitly.
  - CSV/parquet: read the headers; say what columns each contains.
  - Commentary (if generated): one line saying what it is — the
    Neil-style Economic Daily writeup — NOT a restatement of the numbers.
One or two sentences per file, plain prose, <b>bold</b> key labels.
Descriptive only — if it restates the headline numbers, trim it.
Every file named here must appear in email_attachments.txt, and vice
versa.

## Attachments

- outputs/figures/fig1_X.png ← inline in email body
- outputs/processed/table1.csv ← attached, backup
- outputs/email/commentary.md ← attached, backup (only if newsletter
  was generated this run; see "Newsletter commentary")

## Charts in email vs backup

- Inline: fig1, fig2 (Jan 2010 – present)
- Backup: fig3 (full sample), fig4 (sensitivity)
- Commentary: backup attachment, never inline (it's prose, not a figure)

## Newsletter commentary

Only if requested. Use the template at:
@C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/prompts/post_analysis_commentary.md

Write to:
outputs/email/commentary.md

The commentary follows Neil Dutta's Economic Daily style: terse, paragraph
form, no bullets, leading with the finding. Written for a non-technical
reader per "Audience register" — plain language, but the named
model/technique, the sample range, and any decisive assumption must survive
in a sentence or two of prose (the newsletter has no Model details section).
Cited literature actually used is hyperlinked and kept very short. Suggest
ONE title at the end (≤10 words, sentence case, no colon).

After writing commentary.md, wire it into the email so it ships as an
attachment on the SAME email (not a second send):
  1. Append outputs/email/commentary.md to outputs/email/email_attachments.txt
     (one path per line; don't duplicate if already present).
  2. Add its one-line entry to the email's "What's attached" section
     (what it is, not a number restatement — see "Email draft").
Because the Email-draft step already created email_attachments.txt, this
is an append, not a create. After this step, email_draft.md and
email_attachments.txt must still be consistent.

## Send already-drafted (no regeneration)

Trigger: "send drafted".

For when the user has already generated and HAND-EDITED the email files
and wants to send exactly those, untouched.

Do NOT run "Required pre-step", "Email draft", or "Newsletter commentary".
Do NOT re-read outputs/processed to re-derive numbers.
Do NOT overwrite email_draft.md, email_subject.txt, or email_attachments.txt.

1. Verify all three exist and are non-empty:
   outputs/email/{email_draft.md, email_subject.txt, email_attachments.txt}
   If any is missing, STOP — tell the user to run "wrap up" first.
2. Echo the subject, the body's first two lines, and the attachment list
   back for a final visual check. If commentary.md exists on disk but is
   NOT in the attachment list, flag it before sending — the user may have
   meant to include it.
3. Run the send command from "Auto-send" on those exact files.
4. Confirm receipt. On failure, surface the error; do NOT retry.

## Auto-send

Only if the user typed "wrap up + send".

By this point the Email-draft step (and Newsletter-commentary step, if
run) have already written email_subject.txt and email_attachments.txt.
Auto-send only CONSUMES them — it does not create or modify them. If any
of the three files is missing, STOP and re-run the Email-draft step;
do not silently regenerate from the send step.

Run (using the shared venv python explicitly):
  /c/Users/asingh/envs/shared-3.10/Scripts/python \
      /c/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/scripts/send_via_graph.py \
      --subject-file outputs/email/email_subject.txt \
      --body-file outputs/email/email_draft.md \
      --attachments outputs/email/email_attachments.txt \
      --to-env-var ECON_DAILY_RECIPIENTS

The script reads recipient list from $ECON_DAILY_RECIPIENTS (comma-separated).
Credentials come from AS_MSGRAPH_TENANT_ID / CLIENT_ID / CLIENT_SECRET.
The body is always email_draft.md; commentary rides as an attachment,
never as the body.

After running, confirm with the user that they received it. If the send
fails, do NOT retry — surface the error so the user can debug creds.

## What NOT to do

- Don't draft email/commentary before the analysis finishes.
- Don't invent numbers or hand-wave with "approximately" when the
  parquet/CSV is right there on disk.
- Don't strip the named model/technique, the sample range, or the key
  assumptions when simplifying — simplify the language, keep the facts.
  Both deliverables must retain all three.
- Don't write the email body paragraphs at full technical rigor — that's
  the Model details section's job; the body stays accessible. The newsletter
  has NO Model details section.
- Don't fabricate a citation URL — hyperlink only verifiable canonical
  sources; otherwise ship the short cite without a link.
- Don't cite literature that wasn't actually used in the project.
- Don't defer email_subject.txt / email_attachments.txt creation to the
  send step — the Email-draft step writes all three bundle files so every
  wrap-up variant is send-ready.
- Don't auto-send unless explicitly requested ("+ send").
- Don't email files outside outputs/ — only attach what was produced
  by this analysis, never raw data or upstream files.
- Don't send the commentary as a second standalone email — it rides as
  an attachment on the analysis email (per "Newsletter commentary").
